/etc/default/grub (bootloader)

A bootloader in Linux is a small software program that loads the operating system kernel into the computer's memory after the computer finishes turning on. [1, 2, 3] 
## Key Functions of a Linux Bootloader

* Bridges Firmware and Kernel: Takes over control from your computer's startup firmware (such as [BIOS or UEFI](https://www.baeldung.com/linux/bootloader-definition)) once hardware checks finish. [3, 4] 
* Provides a Boot Menu: Displays a list of installed operating systems or different Linux kernel versions so you can pick which one to start. [1, 5, 6] 
* Loads into RAM: Locates the main system files and copies the kernel and temporary root filesystem (initramfs) from your hard drive into the primary memory (RAM). [2, 3] 
* Passes Parameters: Allows you to type or change startup settings and commands before the kernel starts, which helps with troubleshooting. [2, 3, 5] 

## Common Linux Bootloaders

* [GNU GRUB](https://serverspace.us/support/help/what-is-grub-the-boot-loader-without-which-linux-wont-start/) (Grand Unified Bootloader) is the standard and most widely used bootloader on modern desktop Linux computers.
* Syslinux is frequently used for lightweight or portable live-USB media.
* U-Boot is popular for embedded devices like routers and the Raspberry Pi. [1, 2, 5, 7] 

