/etc/modprobe.d/*.conf 
/etc/modules-load.d/*.conf


In Linux, both directories control how kernel modules (drivers) behave, but /etc/modules-load.d/ decides which modules automatically load at boot, while /etc/modprobe.d/ decides how those modules behave (parameters, blacklisting, and aliases) whenever they are loaded. [1, 2] 
## /etc/modules-load.d/ (The "What")
This directory is used by modern service managers like systemd to load a static list of kernel modules during system startup. [3, 4] 

* How it works: You create a file ending in .conf (e.g., /etc/modules-load.d/wireguard.conf) and simply type the module name on a new line.
* Syntax: Plain text. One module name per line. Comments start with #.
* Limitations: You cannot pass arguments, tweak settings, or blacklist modules here. It only triggers a blind, straightforward load. [1, 3, 5, 6] 

## /etc/modprobe.d/ (The "How")
This directory configures the modprobe utility, which is the application responsible for actually inserting and removing modules from the kernel. Files here apply rules whether a module is loaded automatically at boot or manually by a user later on. [7, 8, 9] 
Configuration files in this directory use specific command keywords to achieve four primary functions:

* Passing Options: Fine-tune module behavior by feeding it settings.
* Syntax: options [module_name] [parameter]=[value]
   * Example: options i915 enable_guc=3 (Enables a specific feature on Intel graphics drivers). [1, 10] 
* Blacklisting: Stop specific modules from loading automatically. This is helpful for disabling broken or conflicting hardware drivers.
* Syntax: blacklist [module_name]
   * Example: blacklist nouveau (Commonly used to disable the open-source Nvidia driver when installing proprietary ones). [1, 5] 
* Creating Aliases: Give an alternate name to a module.
* Syntax: alias [alias_name] [real_module_name] [1] 
* Soft Dependencies: Instruct the system to load one module immediately before or after another module.
* Syntax: softdep [module_name] pre: [other_module]

## Summary Table

| Feature | /etc/modules-load.d/*.conf | /etc/modprobe.d/*.conf |
|---|---|---|
| Primary Purpose | Forces modules to load during system boot. | Sets rules, custom parameters, and limits for modules. |
| Triggered By | systemd-modules-load.service. | The modprobe execution tool. |
| Accepts Options? | No. | Yes. |
| Can Blacklist? | No. | Yes. |


